course: biology
lesson: 134
title: Finding Nemo: real sharks, anglerfish, pelicans and gulls
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Bruce the great white promises that fish are friends, not food, but a real great white is a hunter of fish, seals and dead whales.
explain: Still, sharks are not monsters: they very rarely bite people, and most bites are mistakes where the shark lets go, while people kill many millions of sharks every year.
explain: Down in the black deep sea a real anglerfish dangles a glowing lure lit by bacteria, and any small fish that swims up to the light ends up behind teeth that point inwards.
explain: Up at the surface, a pelican scoops fish with a stretchy pouch like a net, and gulls really do squabble for every scrap, even snatching chips from a person's hand.
fact: Great white sharks eat fish, seals and dead whales; fat seals give them the energy to swim fast and keep their bodies warmer than the water around them.
fact: Sharks can smell tiny amounts of blood and other substances in water, but the idea that one drop makes them go crazy at once is exaggerated; their nose is only for smelling, not breathing.
fact: Sharks also sense tiny electric signals from other animals' muscles through little pores on their snouts.
fact: Sharks lose teeth all the time and grow new ones in rows behind, so one shark can use thousands of teeth in its life, and shark teeth are common fossils.
fact: Hammerhead sharks have a wide flat head with an eye at each end, which spreads their senses for a better view and more electric sensors, and they sometimes pin stingrays down with it.
fact: The shortfin mako is the fastest shark, reaching about 50 kilometres an hour or more in bursts, and it can leap several metres out of the water.
fact: Sharks very rarely bite people; people are not their normal food, and each year people kill many millions of sharks while sharks kill only a handful of people.
fact: Deep-sea anglerfish live in the dark deep sea; the glowing lure on the female's head is lit by bacteria living inside it, and in many kinds the males are tiny.
fact: Anglerfish teeth point inwards so prey cannot escape, and their stomachs stretch so some can swallow prey bigger than themselves, because food is rare in the deep.
fact: Pelicans scoop fish and water into their throat pouch, then tip their heads to drain the water before swallowing; a brown pelican's pouch holds about 11 litres, more than its stomach.
fact: Gulls are bold, noisy birds that eat almost anything, steal food from each other and from people, and are more likely to grab food that a person is holding or looking at.
fact: Many reef fish in the film are hard to keep in tanks, needing lots of space, stable clean salt water and the right food; Gill's kind, the Moorish idol, is one of the hardest to keep alive.
exercise: What do real great white sharks eat? | Fish, seals and dead whales | Only seaweed and tiny plankton | Only smaller sharks of other kinds | Nothing, they live on sunlight
exercise: Why are seals such good food for a great white? | Their fat gives lots of energy for speed and warmth | They are the slowest and weakest animals in the whole sea | They have no bones to choke on | They taste of fish
exercise: Does one drop of blood make a shark go crazy at once? | No, they smell blood well but that idea is exaggerated | Yes, every shark goes wild instantly | Yes, even from the other side of the ocean, many days away | No, sharks cannot smell at all
exercise: What else, besides smell, helps a shark find prey? | Pores on its snout that sense tiny electric signals | Big ears on the sides of its head that hear fish breathing | A glowing lure on its head | A sense of taste in its fins
exercise: What happens when a shark loses a tooth? | A new one moves forward from the rows behind | It has a gap there for the rest of its long life | It grows a beak instead | It must stop hunting
exercise: Where are a hammerhead's eyes? | At the two ends of its wide flat head | On top of its head, close together like a frog's | Underneath its belly, close to the front fins | On the end of its tail, looking backwards
exercise: What is a hammerhead's head useful for? | It spreads its senses and can pin down stingrays | Hammering open the hard shells of crabs and clams | Digging holes for nests | Fighting other hammerheads only
exercise: Which is the fastest shark? | The shortfin mako | The great white | The hammerhead | The whale shark
exercise: How often do sharks bite people? | Very rarely, and most bites are mistakes | Every day, on nearly every beach in the world | Never, sharks have no teeth | Whenever a person swims
exercise: Which kills more of the other each year? | People kill many millions of sharks | Sharks kill millions of people every year | They kill about the same number | Neither ever kills the other
exercise: What makes the anglerfish's lure glow? | Bacteria living inside it | A tiny battery in its head | Sunlight it stored in the day | Sparks from its sharp teeth
exercise: Which anglerfish carry the glowing lure? | The females | The young males | Only the babies | Every anglerfish of every age
exercise: Why do anglerfish teeth point inwards? | So prey cannot escape once it is in the mouth | So the teeth do not fall out when it bites hard | So it can smile at other fish | To scrape algae off rocks
exercise: Why can some anglerfish swallow prey bigger than themselves? | Their stomachs stretch, and food is rare in the deep | They chop the prey into small pieces with their sharp fins | They share it with other anglerfish | They only pretend to swallow it
exercise: What does a pelican do after scooping a pouch of fish and water? | Tips its head to drain the water, then swallows | Swallows the water and keeps the fish | Spits out all the fish and drinks the salty water instead | Carries the water home for its chicks
exercise: About how much water can a brown pelican's pouch hold? | About 11 litres, more than its stomach | About 1,000 litres, as much as a small swimming pool | About 100 litres, as much as a full bathtub | About half a cup, no more than a small sip
exercise: Which of your snacks is a gull most likely to grab? | The one you are holding or looking at | The one hidden in your bag | The one you threw away an hour ago | None, gulls never steal from people
exercise: Why is a small tank hard on many reef fish? | They need lots of space, stable clean salt water and the right food | They need fresh water straight from a tap, changed every single morning | They like to be alone in the dark | They only eat other fish's eggs, which are hard to buy
test: «4 gulls screaming mine mine and 2 pelicans on the pier, how many legs» | 12 | 6 | 24 | 8
test: «Bruce said fish are friends not food so sharks are veggie, right» What is right? | No, great whites hunt fish, seals and eat dead whales | Yes, sharks eat only seaweed and the odd jellyfish now | Yes, they gave up meat years ago | No, they eat only people
test: «saw Jaws, gonna never swim again coz sharks hunt people» What is right? | Sharks very rarely bite people, and most bites are mistakes | Sharks hunt people every day, just like in the film | Every beach has a shark waiting just past the waves | Sharks only eat children
test: In Shark Tale, a shark called Lenny refuses to eat fish and becomes a vegetarian. What is true of real great whites? | They need meat, such as fat seals, for energy | Many great whites eat only plants | Great whites can switch to plants when they want | Great whites eat only shrimp
test: «drop of blood and the shark's gonna go mental» What is right? | Sharks smell blood very well, but going wild at one drop is exaggerated | Yes, a single drop drives every shark in the whole sea crazy in a second | No, sharks smell with their fins | No, sharks cannot smell blood at all
test: «shark broke a tooth on a boat lol, toothless now» What is right? | A new tooth soon moves forward from the rows behind | Yes, sharks grow only one set of teeth in their whole lives | Yes, it will now eat only soup | No, shark teeth never break
test: «the light in the deep sea, is it a torch» What makes an anglerfish's lure glow? | Glowing bacteria living inside it | A tiny electric battery | Lava from under the sea | Light stored from the sun
test: What would happen to a small fish that swims up to an anglerfish's light? | It would likely be grabbed, with inward teeth stopping its escape | It would be warmed by the glowing light and then swim off safely home | It would be shown the way home | It would make friends with the anglerfish
test: «that pelican's pouch is full of water, does it drink all of it» What does it really do? | Tips its head to drain the water, then swallows the fish | Drinks all of it every time, as pelicans are always thirsty | Keeps it in the pouch all day | Gives it to the gulls
test: «hammerhead's head is a weird design, total fail» Why is it actually useful? | Eyes and electric sensors spread wide help it find and pin down prey | It uses it to knock over small fishing boats and then steal all the fish | It uses it only to look scary | It is no use at all
test: «we're on the pier with chips, gull's staring at me» What is true? | Gulls are more likely to grab food you are holding or looking at | Gulls eat only fish, so they will leave your chips alone | Gulls are scared of people with chips | Gulls cannot see what you hold
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the diet of the great white shark. | It consumes fish, seals and the carcasses of whales | It subsists entirely upon marine vegetation | It subsists principally upon human beings | It subsists solely upon plankton
exam: Identify the source of the light emitted by the lure of the deep-sea anglerfish. | Luminous bacteria resident within the lure | An organ which stores sunlight during the day | Electric discharge from the muscles of the fish | Light reflected from the surface of the sea
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relative mortality as between sharks and human beings? | Human beings kill many millions of sharks annually, whereas sharks kill a very small number of persons | Sharks kill many millions of persons annually, whereas human beings kill very few sharks | The numbers killed on each side are approximately equal | Neither party is ever responsible for the death of the other
exam: Ascertain the manner in which a pelican separates fish from the water collected in its pouch. | The head is tilted so that the water drains away before the fish are swallowed | The water is swallowed and the fish are subsequently regurgitated | The fish are released and the water retained for drinking | The water is retained in the pouch for the provision of the young
